UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$The Inner Operating System$v$, subtitle = $v$Why the strongest habits still leak without identity, attention and state$v$, summary = $v$Discipline trains the hardware. This shelf trains the software: the self-image you act from, the attention you aim, and the state you operate in. This is the map.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$promising$v$, protocol = $v${}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$Explains why willpower alone plateaus$v$, $v$The three-layer model every lesson on the shelf builds on$v$, $v$Honest expectations set: evidence and speculation named as such$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$A model, not a finding; the three layers are a way of sorting the lessons, and the evidence sits in the lessons$v$, $v$Not a substitute for therapy where mood or anxiety is the problem$v$], body_md = $v$**Why willpower plateaus**
You can force a habit for thirty days on willpower. You cannot force it for ten years. What carries behaviour across years is not effort; it is the inner operating system underneath the effort, and this shelf works on its three layers.

**Identity, the self-image you act from**
People behave in ways that feel consistent with who they believe they are. A person who sees themselves as an athlete does not negotiate with the alarm the way a person who is trying to work out does. Lessons 8 and 2 work here.

**Attention, what the mind is primed to notice**
You do not experience the world; you experience what you attend to. Two people walk the same street, one primed for openings and one for threats, and each finds what they were primed for. Attention is trainable. Lessons 2, 3, 4 and 6 work here.

**State, the energy you operate in**
The same task is easy in a resourced state and impossible in a depleted one. Physiology, breath, posture, movement and light, is the fastest lever on state, far faster than thinking your way into a different feeling. Lessons 5, 7 and 9 work here.

**The honesty contract**
This shelf takes the so-called soft topics seriously, manifestation, energy, self-image, and tells you the truth about each: what controlled research supports, what is promising, and what is speculative. Every lesson carries an evidence chip. Nothing here asks you to believe anything mystical. Everything here works through attention, identity and physiology, which you already own.

**How to read this shelf**
Ten lessons in order, the last one a recap that turns the nine into a daily rhythm. Train the body and run the protocols on the other shelves; this is the system they run on.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$Every habit, workout and check-in in this app runs on top of your self-image, your attention and your state. If those leak, effort leaks. This lesson is the map the next nine fill in.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Write one sentence: the person I am becoming is. You will refine it in lesson 8.$v$, $v$Notice today which of the three layers, identity, attention or state, leaks most for you.$v$, $v$Read lesson 2 tomorrow.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Behaviour that lasts is identity-consistent, not willpower-forced.$v$, $v$You act on what you attend to, and attention is trainable.$v$, $v$State beats motivation: change the physiology first and the feeling follows.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "What carries behaviour over years, in this model?", "choices": ["An identity the behaviour is consistent with", "Stricter tracking", "Maximum daily willpower"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Willpower is a short-term override. Consistency over years comes from acting like the person you believe you are."}, {"q": "Which is the fastest lever on your operating state?", "choices": ["Positive thoughts", "Physiology: breath, posture, movement", "Waiting for motivation"], "correct": 1, "explain": "The body changes state faster than the mind. Two minutes of physiology outruns an hour of trying to think yourself into a mood."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Kross, E.", "title": "Chatter: The Voice in Our Head, Why It Matters, and How to Harness It", "year": 2021}, {"author": "Markus, H. & Nurius, P.", "title": "Possible selves (Am. Psychol.)", "year": 1986}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$Which of the three layers, identity, attention or state, is the one you have never worked on directly?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$A week of watching the three layers. Which one leaked, and on which day?$v$, practice_minutes = 5, lesson_number = 1, display_order = 1, course_role = $v$foundations$v$, read_time_min = 2, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'inner-operating-system';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Manifestation, Demystified$v$, subtitle = $v$The three real mechanisms, and the one claim the evidence does not support$v$, summary = $v$Manifestation works for many people, and not the way the books say. Three mechanisms are real, one claim is not, and the honest version is a daily ritual of attention and identity with an action attached.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$speculative$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "2 min a morning", "frequency": "Daily", "intensity": "One desire in present-tense detail, one same-day action", "prerequisites": "None"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$Keeps the useful core of manifestation$v$, $v$Removes the wish-and-wait failure mode$v$, $v$Turns a vision into primed attention and a same-day action$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$Positive fantasy without action lowers effort in controlled studies; never run the ritual without the step$v$, $v$The metaphysical claim has no controlled evidence; the chip says speculative on purpose$v$], body_md = $v$**Something real, not magic**
Millions of people say manifestation changed their lives. Dismissing all of them is lazy, and swallowing the law of attraction whole is worse. The honest position is that something real is happening, and it is not magic. Three mechanisms carry it.

**Primed attention**
Define a desire vividly and revisit it daily, and the attention system starts flagging openings it used to filter out. Decide to buy a certain car and you see it everywhere; the car did not multiply, the filter changed. People who write down what they want spot the aligned conversation, job post or person because the target is loaded in attention.

**Identity-consistent behaviour**
Repeatedly imagining yourself as a certain person shifts the self-concept, and you start making the hundred small choices that person makes. The "vibration" people report is usually this: they carry themselves differently, speak differently, and others respond. Markus and Nurius (1986) studied it as possible selves: a vivid future self changes present behaviour.

**Action bias**
People who expect good outcomes take more shots: they apply, ask, show up, start. More attempts, more hits. Wiseman (2003) found that self-described lucky people create and notice more chance opportunities through openness and action, not fortune.

**What the evidence does not support**
There is no controlled evidence that thoughts alone alter external events, that the universe rearranges outcomes to match a frequency. That is the speculative part, and this lesson wears its chip on purpose.

**The trap**
Kappes and Oettingen (2011) showed that positive fantasy alone, enjoying the dream as if achieved, measurably lowers the energy and effort people put toward the goal. Passive manifestation is not neutral. It can work against you.

**The honest ritual**
Write the desire in present-tense detail; that loads the filter. Revisit it for two minutes every morning, because priming decays. The same day, take one action that a person with that outcome would take. That is where the law actually operates, and you need believe nothing unprovable to use it.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$Manifestation is one of the most searched ideas in self-development, and most people practise the version the research says backfires: dream, feel good, wait. Keep the three mechanisms and it becomes a daily tool.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Write your one clearest desire in present-tense detail, two minutes.$v$, $v$Do one thing today that the version of you who already has it would do.$v$, $v$Notice one coincidence today and ask whether your filter caught it because it was primed.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Real: primed attention, identity-consistent behaviour, action bias.$v$, $v$Not evidenced: thoughts alone changing external events.$v$, $v$Positive fantasy without action lowers drive; always pair the vision with a same-day step.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "Which is a real, evidence-plausible mechanism behind manifestation?", "choices": ["Wanting something hard enough", "Thoughts emitting a frequency the universe answers", "Primed attention noticing aligned openings"], "correct": 2, "explain": "Defining a target vividly changes what the attention system flags. The filter changes, not the world."}, {"q": "What did Kappes and Oettingen find about positive fantasy alone?", "choices": ["It measurably lowers energy and effort toward the goal", "It doubles achievement", "It has no effect"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Enjoying the dream as if achieved relaxes the system; the drive to close the gap drops. Vision needs an action attached."}, {"q": "Why does this lesson carry a speculative chip?", "choices": ["Attention priming is unproven", "The claim that thoughts change external events has no controlled evidence", "Visualisation is pseudoscience"], "correct": 1, "explain": "The mechanisms are ordinary psychology. The metaphysical causality claim is the part without evidence, and the chip says so."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Oettingen, G.", "title": "Rethinking Positive Thinking: Inside the New Science of Motivation", "year": 2014}, {"author": "Kappes, H. B. & Oettingen, G.", "title": "Positive fantasies about idealized futures sap energy (J. Exp. Soc. Psychol.)", "year": 2011}, {"author": "Wiseman, R.", "title": "The Luck Factor", "year": 2003}, {"author": "Markus, H. & Nurius, P.", "title": "Possible selves (Am. Psychol.)", "year": 1986}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$What do you want most, and when did you last do one thing about it on the same day you thought about it?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$A week of the two-minute ritual with the action attached. What did the filter catch, and what did you do about it?$v$, practice_minutes = 5, lesson_number = 2, display_order = 2, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 2, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'manifestation-demystified';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$WOOP: Wishes That Survive Contact With Reality$v$, subtitle = $v$The evidence-based cousin of manifestation: Wish, Outcome, Obstacle, Plan$v$, summary = $v$Twenty years of research in a five-minute mental protocol that roughly doubles follow-through. Dream it, feel it, then collide it with the obstacle inside you and pre-load the response.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$strong$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "5 min", "frequency": "One WOOP per goal; re-run when the obstacle changes", "intensity": "Wish, outcome, inner obstacle, if-then plan", "prerequisites": "None"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$Meta-analyses behind it across health, study and fitness goals$v$, $v$Five minutes and a quiet moment$v$, $v$Turns vague wishes into automatic if-then responses$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$The obstacle must be inside you; an external one produces a plan you cannot run$v$, $v$A WOOP on a wish you do not actually want produces a very efficient way to do the wrong thing$v$], body_md = $v$**The fix for lesson 2's trap**
Lesson 2 ended with a warning: fantasy without friction backfires. This lesson is the fix, and it is one of the best-evidenced motivation tools in psychology. WOOP stands for Wish, Outcome, Obstacle, Plan. Oettingen (2014) developed it as mental contrasting with implementation intentions and tested it in dozens of controlled studies: more exercise, better eating, better grades, faster recovery habits. The if-then half alone carries a meta-analysis of 94 studies with a medium to large effect on goal attainment (Gollwitzer and Sheeran 2006), and Duckworth et al. (2013) showed the combination working in adolescents' schoolwork.

**Wish**
Name one meaningful wish for the coming weeks, challenging but feasible. Train four times a week.

**Outcome**
Imagine the best result vividly for sixty seconds: how it feels, what changes. Indulge fully. This is the manifestation muscle, used on purpose.

**Obstacle**
Now the turn most people never make: find the obstacle inside you. Not the schedule, but the drained feeling at 18:00 that opens the phone. Feel that scene honestly. The collision of dream against inner obstacle is mental contrasting, and it is what converts a pleasant fantasy into mobilised energy.

**Plan**
Pre-load the response as an if-then. If it is 18:00 and I feel drained and reach for my phone, then I put on my training shoes and walk out the door. If-then plans work because they hand the behaviour to the situation cue; the decision is already made when the moment arrives.

**Why the order matters**
Obstacle before dream kills belief. Dream before obstacle without the collision is the trap. Wish, Outcome, Obstacle, Plan uses the dream to energise and the obstacle to aim. Run one per goal, re-run when the obstacle changes, and pre-load tonight the habit you will lock tomorrow.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$This is the strongest-evidenced protocol on the shelf, the tool that makes every vision from lessons 2 and 4 executable. Five minutes of WOOP routinely beats hours of raw motivation.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Run one full WOOP, five minutes, on your most important current goal.$v$, $v$Write the if-then plan where you will see it tomorrow.$v$, $v$Tonight, WOOP tomorrow's hardest habit before sleep.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Wish, Outcome, Obstacle, Plan, in that order.$v$, $v$The obstacle is internal: the feeling or thought that actually derails you.$v$, $v$If-then plans hand the behaviour to the situation; the decision is made in advance.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "What makes WOOP different from pure visualisation?", "choices": ["It uses affirmations", "It skips imagining the outcome", "It collides the dream with your inner obstacle, then pre-loads a plan"], "correct": 2, "explain": "Mental contrasting converts fantasy into directed energy; the if-then plan makes the response automatic."}, {"q": "A well-formed WOOP obstacle is…", "choices": ["The drained feeling at 18:00 that sends me to my phone", "Bad weather", "My boss and my schedule"], "correct": 0, "explain": "The operative obstacle is the specific feeling, thought or impulse inside you. That is what a plan can be written against."}, {"q": "Why do if-then plans work?", "choices": ["They increase motivation", "They delegate the decision to the situation cue in advance", "They are written down"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Gollwitzer and Sheeran 2006: deciding in advance removes the in-the-moment negotiation where goals usually die."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Oettingen, G.", "title": "Rethinking Positive Thinking: Inside the New Science of Motivation", "year": 2014}, {"author": "Gollwitzer, P. M. & Sheeran, P.", "title": "Implementation intentions and goal achievement: a meta-analysis of effects and processes (Adv. Exp. Soc. Psychol.)", "year": 2006}, {"author": "Duckworth, A. L., Kirby, T. A., Gollwitzer, A. & Oettingen, G.", "title": "From fantasy to action: mental contrasting with implementation intentions (MCII) improves academic performance in children (Soc. Psychol. Personal. Sci.)", "year": 2013}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$What is the obstacle inside you that most often wins, and at what time of day does it win?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$A week with one if-then plan written down. Did the cue arrive, and did the plan fire?$v$, practice_minutes = 5, lesson_number = 3, display_order = 3, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 2, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'woop-mental-contrasting';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Visualisation That Works$v$, subtitle = $v$Process over outcome, all senses, obstacles included$v$, summary = $v$Elite sport has used structured imagery for decades. Rehearsing the process improves performance; rehearsing only the outcome can lower it. The difference is one rule, and this is the working protocol.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$promising$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "3 to 5 min", "frequency": "Before training or a hard block", "intensity": "First person, real time, the hardest moment included", "prerequisites": "None"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$Borrowed from sports psychology and works for any hard task$v$, $v$Skill and confidence without extra physical load$v$, $v$Five minutes, best right before the hard thing$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$Outcome-only imagery lowered performance in the classic study; always rehearse the doing$v$, $v$Imagery is rehearsal, not preparation; it sits on top of the actual practice$v$], body_md = $v$**What the research supports**
Motor imagery uses overlapping neural machinery with physical execution, and structured imagery programmes reliably improve performance in athletes. How you visualise decides whether it helps.

**The critical study**
Pham and Taylor (1999) split students before an exam. One group visualised getting the A, the outcome. One group visualised the process: sitting down, studying, working through the material. The process group studied more and scored higher. The outcome-only group did worse than the controls. It is the trap from lesson 2 again: consume the reward in imagination and the drive to earn it relaxes.

**Rule one: rehearse the doing, not the podium.**

**The PETTLEP checklist**
Holmes and Collins (2001) built it for applied sport psychology, to make imagery vivid enough to transfer. Physical: imagine in the body, the grip, the breath, the cold air, through your own eyes. Environment: your actual gym, desk or kitchen. Task: the specific movements and decisions, step by step. Timing: real time, not fast-forward. Learning: update the image as the skill grows. Emotion: include the nerves, the effort, the satisfaction of doing it. Perspective: first person as the default.

**Rule two: include the obstacle.**
The most useful rep is imagining the moment it gets hard, rep eight burning, the urge to check the phone, the craving at 21:00, and seeing yourself execute anyway. That is mental contrasting fused with imagery, and it is what separates preparation from daydreaming.

**The protocol, three to five minutes**
Close the eyes, three slow breaths. Run the task in first person with PETTLEP vividness. Insert the hardest moment and watch yourself handle it. End at completion, the last rep done, the block finished. Then go and do it. Visualisation is rehearsal, not wish-granting. Rehearse the performance and the performance improves.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$Done wrong, outcome only, visualisation measurably hurts follow-through. Done right, process plus obstacles, it is one of the cheapest performance aids there is. The difference is one rule.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Before today's training or hardest task: three minutes of first-person process imagery.$v$, $v$Include one obstacle moment and watch yourself execute through it.$v$, $v$End at completion, then start.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Process imagery improves performance; outcome-only can reduce it.$v$, $v$PETTLEP vividness, body, environment, real time, emotion, is what makes imagery transfer.$v$, $v$Always rehearse the hardest moment and your response to it.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "Which imagery improved exam results in the classic study?", "choices": ["Visualising the celebration afterwards", "Visualising the top grade", "Visualising the process of studying"], "correct": 2, "explain": "Pham and Taylor 1999: process simulation increased studying; outcome-only let students consume the reward and do worse."}, {"q": "What does the second T in PETTLEP stand for, and why?", "choices": ["Timing: image in real time, not fast-forward", "Tension: stay stressed", "Talent: imagine being gifted"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Real-time imagery keeps the rehearsal faithful to execution, which is what makes it transfer."}, {"q": "The single most valuable image to rehearse is…", "choices": ["The finish line", "The hardest moment, with you executing anyway", "Your competition failing"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Pre-living the obstacle and your response is rehearsal for the exact moment goals are usually lost."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Pham, L. B. & Taylor, S. E.", "title": "From thought to action: effects of process- versus outcome-based mental simulations on performance (Pers. Soc. Psychol. Bull.)", "year": 1999}, {"author": "Holmes, P. S. & Collins, D. J.", "title": "The PETTLEP approach to motor imagery: a functional equivalence model for sport psychologists (J. Appl. Sport Psychol.)", "year": 2001}, {"author": "Cumming, J. & Williams, S. E.", "title": "The role of imagery in performance (Oxford Handbook of Sport and Performance Psychology)", "year": 2012}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$When you picture the thing you want, do you see yourself doing it, or having done it?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$A week of three minutes before the hard thing. Which rehearsed obstacle actually showed up, and what did you do?$v$, practice_minutes = 4, lesson_number = 4, display_order = 4, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 2, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'visualization-that-works';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Elevating Your Energy: State Management$v$, subtitle = $v$Your energy is physiology plus mood plus meaning, and all three are trainable$v$, summary = $v$When people say raise your energy, something real sits underneath: operating state. It is made of arousal, mood and a sense of meaning, and it moves in minutes with breath, posture, light, music and an honest audit.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$promising$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "2 to 5 min", "frequency": "Before anything hard; the audit weekly", "intensity": "Breath, posture, one track, one line of meaning", "prerequisites": "None"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$State shifts measured in minutes, not weeks$v$, $v$An energy audit that finds the hidden drains$v$, $v$A repeatable ritual before hard blocks$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$The fast up-shift breathing is hyperventilation: never standing, never in or near water, not with a heart condition or in pregnancy, and stop if the hands tingle$v$, $v$State management is not a substitute for sleep; a flat week usually means the Recovery shelf, not this one$v$], body_md = $v$**The honest translation**
"High-vibe" is not a frequency the body emits. But everyone knows the difference between a day at full capacity and a day at 40%. That difference is operating state, measurable in arousal, mood and focus, and far more controllable than most people think. Energy is physiological arousal times mood times a sense of meaning. Raise any one and the others tend to follow.

**Breath, one to two minutes**
The physiological sigh, two inhales through the nose and a slow exhale through the mouth, is the quickest validated down-shift; the Mind shelf owns the dose and the Stanford trial (Balban et al. 2023). To shift up: twenty to thirty fast, full breaths, then one long exhale. That is deliberate hyperventilation, so sit down for it, never near water, never with a heart condition or in pregnancy, and stop if the hands tingle. Calm on demand, activation on demand, with the safety line attached to the second.

**Posture and movement, two to five minutes**
Peper and Lin (2012) had people walk slumped or upright and rate their energy: slumped fed low-energy, negative thoughts, and upright supported energy and confidence. Better still, move. Two minutes of fast stairs, squats or a walk outside beats a coffee for felt energy. Emotion follows motion.

**Light and environment**
Morning outdoor light anchors the clock and is the largest background variable in daily energy; the Recovery shelf has the dose. Spaces are triggers too: the cluttered desk, the phone on the table, each a small, constant drain.

**Music and ritual**
Music shifts arousal and mood within minutes, which is why every changing room uses it. Build a three-song activation list that means one thing: we are switching on.

**The energy audit, once, then weekly**
Two columns. Chargers: people, places, activities after which your energy is up. Drains: after which it is down, the scroll, certain conversations, skipped meals, 23:30 bedtimes. Schedule one more charger and delete one drain this week. Nothing mystical; ruthless accounting.

**The peak-state ritual, before anything hard**
Ninety seconds of breath, up or down as needed. Posture: stand tall, shoulders back, thirty seconds. One activation track. One sentence of meaning, why this block matters. Then begin. You cannot always choose your circumstances. You can almost always choose the state you take into them.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$State is the multiplier on everything else in this app. The same workout, work block or conversation lands completely differently at 40% and at 90%, and these levers move state in minutes.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Five physiological sighs now; rate your state before and after.$v$, $v$Write the energy audit: three chargers, three drains. Cut one drain this week.$v$, $v$Build a three-song activation list for training blocks.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Energy is arousal times mood times meaning, and all three are trainable.$v$, $v$Breath is the fastest lever: the sigh down, fast breathing up, with the safety line.$v$, $v$Audit chargers and drains weekly; schedule the first, delete the second.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "The honest translation of raising your energy is…", "choices": ["Drinking more caffeine", "Emitting a higher frequency", "Shifting arousal, mood and meaning, your operating state"], "correct": 2, "explain": "The felt experience is real. The mechanism is physiology and psychology, which is what makes it trainable."}, {"q": "What is the safety rule for the fast up-shift breathing?", "choices": ["Sit down, never near water, not with a heart condition or in pregnancy, stop if the hands tingle", "Do it standing for effect", "None needed"], "correct": 0, "explain": "It is deliberate hyperventilation. People have drowned doing it in water; the rule is not optional."}, {"q": "The energy audit asks you to…", "choices": ["Rank your friends", "List chargers and drains, then schedule and delete accordingly", "Track calories"], "correct": 1, "explain": "State management is mostly accounting: add what charges you, remove what drains you."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Balban, M. Y. et al.", "title": "Brief structured respiration practices enhance mood and reduce physiological arousal (Cell Rep. Med.)", "year": 2023}, {"author": "Peper, E. & Lin, I.-M.", "title": "Increase or decrease depression: how body postures influence your energy level (Biofeedback)", "year": 2012}, {"author": "Thayer, R. E.", "title": "The Biopsychology of Mood and Arousal", "year": 1989}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$At what percentage did you run today, and what decided it before 09:00?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$A week of the audit and the ritual. Which drain did you cut, and what came back?$v$, practice_minutes = 5, lesson_number = 5, display_order = 5, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 3, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'elevate-your-energy';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Gratitude and Savouring$v$, subtitle = $v$Five-minute practices that reliably raise baseline mood, no journal aesthetic required$v$, summary = $v$Gratitude is one of the most replicated interventions in positive psychology, and savouring is its underrated sibling. Together they retrain the attention filter toward what is working.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$promising$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "3 min evening; 30 s in the moment", "frequency": "Daily; one expressed thank-you a week", "intensity": "Three good things with the why; savour one moment; thank one person", "prerequisites": "None"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$Better mood and life satisfaction in controlled trials$v$, $v$A correction for the negativity bias at the attention level$v$, $v$Under five minutes, and it pairs with the evening check-in$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$Forced gratitude on a bad day backfires; write three small real things instead$v$, $v$Not a treatment for depression; if the evening list is empty for weeks, talk to someone$v$], body_md = $v$**Correcting the filter**
The attention system has a built-in negativity bias: threats and problems are flagged first, wins are filtered out. Useful for survival, bad for baseline mood. Gratitude practice is not positive thinking. It is manually correcting the filter.

**The evidence, briefly**
Emmons and McCullough (2003) had people write weekly gratitude lists or weekly hassles; the gratitude group reported better mood, fewer physical complaints and more exercise. Seligman et al. (2005) tested three good things, write three things that went well tonight and why, against a placebo task; the gains in happiness were still measurable six months later. The effects are moderate, replicated and cheap. Bryant and Veroff (2007) built the research on savouring, the live version, and linked it to higher life satisfaction.

**Three good things, evening, three minutes**
Tonight, write three things that went well. The rule that makes it work: be specific and name the why. Not "training" but "I showed up for the 4×4 at 18:00 although the sofa was winning, because the plan was written down." Specificity forces attention to re-scan the day for wins, and that re-scan is the training.

**Savouring, in the moment, thirty seconds**
Gratitude looks back; savouring happens live. A few times a day, when something is good, the first coffee, the post-training buzz, the evening light, stop for thirty seconds and let it register fully. Name it: this is a good moment. You are teaching attention that good moments are worth flagging.

**Gratitude aimed at people, weekly**
The largest effects in the literature come from expressed gratitude: tell one person, concretely, what they did and what it meant. One message a week.

**Why it sits on this shelf**
Lesson 2 taught that a primed filter finds what it seeks. Gratitude is the same priming aimed at what is already working, the best-evidenced abundance practice there is. The Mind shelf's five-minute journal is the compact daily version of the same habit.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$Baseline mood is trainable, and the training is embarrassingly simple: point attention at what worked, repeatedly. The evening habit doubles as fuel for the check-in streak.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Tonight: three good things, each with the why.$v$, $v$Savour one moment today for a full thirty seconds.$v$, $v$Send one concrete thank-you message this week.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Gratitude corrects the negativity bias at the filter; it is attention training.$v$, $v$Specific and causal beats generic; expressed gratitude beats private lists.$v$, $v$Savouring is live gratitude: thirty seconds of letting a good moment register.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "What is gratitude practice actually training?", "choices": ["Memory capacity", "Politeness", "The attention filter, re-scanning for what worked"], "correct": 2, "explain": "The nightly re-scan for wins counters the negativity bias; over weeks the filter itself shifts."}, {"q": "What makes a three-good-things entry work?", "choices": ["Being specific and naming why it went well", "Listing at least ten items", "A nice notebook"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Specificity plus the cause is what the validated protocol requires; it forces genuine re-scanning."}, {"q": "Which form of gratitude shows the largest effects?", "choices": ["Private mental gratitude", "Expressed gratitude aimed at a real person", "Gratitude quotes online"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Concrete thanks to a real person produces the biggest and longest-lasting boosts, for both parties."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Emmons, R. A. & McCullough, M. E.", "title": "Counting blessings versus burdens: an experimental investigation of gratitude and subjective well-being in daily life (J. Pers. Soc. Psychol.)", "year": 2003}, {"author": "Seligman, M. E. P., Steen, T. A., Park, N. & Peterson, C.", "title": "Positive psychology progress: empirical validation of interventions (Am. Psychol.)", "year": 2005}, {"author": "Bryant, F. B. & Veroff, J.", "title": "Savoring: A New Model of Positive Experience", "year": 2007}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$What went well today that you had already forgotten by dinner?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$A week of three good things. Did the wins start registering during the day, before the notebook?$v$, practice_minutes = 3, lesson_number = 6, display_order = 6, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 2, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'gratitude-savoring';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Inner Dialogue: Coaching Yourself From a Distance$v$, subtitle = $v$Turn the inner critic into an inner coach with one linguistic move$v$, summary = $v$The voice in your head runs commentary all day. Ethan Kross's research shows a simple upgrade: address yourself by name, from the outside, and regulation improves measurably.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$promising$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "30 s in the moment", "frequency": "At every stress spike; the critic audit once", "intensity": "Second or third person, by name; keep the standard, drop the contempt", "prerequisites": "None"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$A one-word technique with lab-measured effects on stress and performance$v$, $v$Rumination loops turned into coach-like problem solving$v$, $v$Pairs with pre-training and pre-conversation nerves$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$Distance is not detachment; if the coach voice goes cold, the standard has replaced the support$v$, $v$Not a treatment for depression or an anxiety disorder$v$], body_md = $v$**Critic, catastrophiser or coach**
You already talk to yourself constantly. The only question is which of the three the voice is.

**The chatter problem**
Under stress, inner speech collapses into first-person loops: why did I do that, what if I fail, I always do this. Kross (2021) calls it chatter, and it measurably degrades problem solving, sleep and performance.

**The fix: distance through language**
In Kross et al. (2014), people preparing for a stressful task who coached themselves in the second or third person, by name, showed calmer physiological profiles, less rumination afterwards and better-rated performance than those using "I". Orvell et al. (2021) found the effect held across a range of intense experiences. The mechanism: your name, or "you", activates the perspective you use when advising a friend, and everyone is wiser about their friends' problems than their own.

**Named coaching, in the moment**
When you notice the spiral, address yourself by name and ask what you would tell a friend in exactly this spot. Then say it, to yourself, by name. Thirty seconds.

**Temporal distancing**
Ask how you will feel about this in a week, and in a year. Zooming the timeline out reliably shrinks the emotional charge of the present problem.

**The critic audit, once**
Write down the three sentences your inner critic repeats most. Read them aloud. Would you say them to someone you were coaching? Rewrite each as the coach version, same standard, zero contempt. "You are behind, as always" becomes "You are behind on this one; what is the first ten-minute move?" Keep the three rewrites visible.

**The standard stays high**
This is not self-soothing or lowering the bar. Great coaches are demanding and on your side. That combination, high standards and high support, is what distanced self-talk makes available to you, from you.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$The tone of your inner dialogue is the tone of your whole day, and it leaks into training, willpower and sleep. Distanced self-talk is the cheapest measured upgrade to that tone.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$At the next stress spike, coach yourself by name for thirty seconds before acting.$v$, $v$Write your critic's top three lines and rewrite them as coach lines.$v$, $v$Before training, one named instruction: your name, first set, nothing else.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$First-person chatter degrades regulation; distance restores it.$v$, $v$Coach yourself by name, or as you: the friend-advice perspective.$v$, $v$High standards and high support: rewrite the critic, keep the bar.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "What did Kross's lab find about third-person self-talk under stress?", "choices": ["It only works for extroverts", "It feels silly and does nothing", "It improves regulation, rumination and rated performance"], "correct": 2, "explain": "The linguistic shift creates psychological distance, the wise perspective you use for friends, with measurable effects."}, {"q": "The coach voice differs from the critic voice by…", "choices": ["Keeping the standard and removing the contempt", "Ignoring mistakes", "Lowering the standard"], "correct": 0, "explain": "High standards with high support. The rewrite keeps accountability and adds a first actionable step."}, {"q": "Temporal distancing means asking…", "choices": ["Why does this always happen to me?", "How will this look in a week, or a year?", "Who is to blame?"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Zooming out the timeline shrinks the present problem's charge and restores perspective."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Kross, E. et al.", "title": "Self-talk as a regulatory mechanism: how you do it matters (J. Pers. Soc. Psychol.)", "year": 2014}, {"author": "Kross, E.", "title": "Chatter: The Voice in Our Head, Why It Matters, and How to Harness It", "year": 2021}, {"author": "Orvell, A. et al.", "title": "Does distanced self-talk facilitate emotion regulation across a range of emotionally intense experiences? (Clin. Psychol. Sci.)", "year": 2021}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$Write the sentence your inner critic said most recently. Would you say it to someone you coach?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$A week of coaching yourself by name. Which line did you use, and what did it change in the moment?$v$, practice_minutes = 3, lesson_number = 7, display_order = 7, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 2, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'distanced-self-talk';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Authentic Self-Image: Who Are You Becoming?$v$, subtitle = $v$Identity is not found; it is built, one vote at a time$v$, summary = $v$The deepest lever in behaviour change is the sentence after I am. This lesson turns your self-image from an inherited accident into a deliberate, honest construction, anchored to chosen values and cast daily.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$promising$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "10 min once; 1 min daily", "frequency": "The values filter once; the I am statement read daily", "intensity": "Five values against last week's hours; one votable sentence", "prerequisites": "None"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$Habits connected to the person they are building$v$, $v$A values exercise that filters goals you never chose$v$, $v$The I am statement your coach uses daily, upgraded$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$An identity claim that outruns the evidence collapses into noise; keep it votable today$v$, $v$Values chosen to impress an audience are not yours; the audit will show it$v$], body_md = $v$**Everything points here**
Attention, state, self-talk: they all run on top of one thing, who you believe you are.

**Self-image is evidence-based, in both senses**
Bem (1972) proposed self-perception theory: we infer who we are largely by watching what we do. Each action is a data point, a vote, for some identity. Skip training: a vote for someone who skips. Show up tired: a vote for someone who shows up. Your self-image is the running tally, which is why the check-in streak in this app is more than points. It is a stack of identity evidence.

**Possible selves**
Markus and Nurius (1986) showed that a vivid possible self, a concrete image of who you could become, organises present behaviour. A vague wish to be better does nothing; a specific version of you who trains four times a week and speaks calmly under pressure gives today's choices a direction to vote for.

**Authentic does not mean fixed**
Finding your authentic self often hides an assumption: that there is a finished self buried somewhere, waiting. The better-supported frame is that authenticity means your actions align with values you chose, not with a fixed essence and not with an audience. You are allowed to build the self. What makes it authentic is that you chose the blueprint.

**The values filter, ten minutes, once**
List the five things you claim to value most: health, family, craft, freedom, courage. Audit last week: where did the hours, money and energy actually go? The gaps are the work. Then check each goal you are chasing: is it yours, or inherited from parents, feeds or comparison? Cut one goal that fails the test. Pursuing borrowed goals is the deepest energy drain there is.

**Write the I am**
One or two sentences, present tense, anchored to values, honest enough that you can act on them today. I am an athlete who keeps promises to himself; calm under pressure, first to move. Avoid inflation: the statement must be one your daily votes can support. Claims that outrun evidence collapse into noise. Claims that grow with evidence become unshakeable. Sheldon and Lyubomirsky (2006) found that writing a best possible self raised positive mood, and the version that lasts is the one you can vote for tomorrow.

**Close the loop in the app**
Your coach already asks for this. Open Settings, then Athlete Profile, then the line that asks who you are becoming, and rewrite it with what you built here. It anchors your daily check-in and the coach's guidance from tomorrow morning.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$Identity is the deepest lever: it decides what feels like you before willpower gets a vote. Making the self-image deliberate, and anchoring it in the app, turns every habit into evidence.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Run the values filter: five claimed values against where last week actually went.$v$, $v$Cut one inherited goal that is not truly yours.$v$, $v$Rewrite your I am statement in Settings, Athlete Profile, so that it is votable today.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$You infer who you are from what you do; every action is a vote.$v$, $v$A vivid, specific possible self organises today's behaviour; vague wishes do not.$v$, $v$Authentic means aligned with chosen values, not a fixed buried essence.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "In self-perception theory, self-image is built mainly from…", "choices": ["What others say about you", "Childhood only", "Observing your own actions, each one a vote"], "correct": 2, "explain": "Bem: we infer who we are from what we repeatedly do. The tally of votes is the self-image, which makes it changeable."}, {"q": "What makes a self-image authentic in this lesson's frame?", "choices": ["Your actions align with values you consciously chose", "Other people approve of it", "It matches a fixed inner essence you discovered"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Authenticity as chosen-values alignment lets you build the self deliberately without faking anything."}, {"q": "Why should the I am statement avoid inflation?", "choices": ["Modesty is polite", "Identity claims must be supportable by today's votes or they collapse", "Short sentences are easier to remember"], "correct": 1, "explain": "An identity that grows with evidence becomes solid; one that outruns evidence reads as noise even to yourself."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Bem, D. J.", "title": "Self-perception theory (Adv. Exp. Soc. Psychol.)", "year": 1972}, {"author": "Markus, H. & Nurius, P.", "title": "Possible selves (Am. Psychol.)", "year": 1986}, {"author": "Sheldon, K. M. & Lyubomirsky, S.", "title": "How to increase and sustain positive emotion: the effects of expressing gratitude and visualizing best possible selves (J. Posit. Psychol.)", "year": 2006}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$Finish the sentence honestly: the person my last seven days voted for is.$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$A week with the I am statement read each morning. Which vote was hardest to cast, and did you cast it?$v$, practice_minutes = 10, lesson_number = 8, display_order = 8, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 3, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'authentic-self-image';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Letting Go: Acceptance and Defusion$v$, subtitle = $v$Control the controllable and release the grip on the rest, as a trained skill$v$, summary = $v$The traditions call it surrender; clinical psychology calls it acceptance and defusion and has two decades of trials behind it. Fighting a thought amplifies it. Here is what works instead.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$promising$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "10 s to 3 min", "frequency": "Defusion any time; the control sort when gripped; the release every evening", "intensity": "I am having the thought that; two columns; three sighs and one line", "prerequisites": "None"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$Backed by the acceptance and commitment therapy trials$v$, $v$Stops the suppression rebound that feeds cravings and rumination$v$, $v$An evening ritual that closes the day instead of carrying it$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$Acceptance is not approval and not passivity; the sort always ends in an action$v$, $v$Trauma or severe anxiety: these skills are paced by a therapist, not learned alone$v$], body_md = $v$**Surrender, done right**
Some of the most useful advice the traditions ever gave, let go, surrender the outcome, sounds like giving up. Done right, it is the opposite: it frees the energy you were spending on an unwinnable fight and returns it to the part you control.

**Why fighting thoughts fails**
Wegner (1994) ran the white bear experiments: told not to think of a white bear, people thought of it more, and the rebound persisted after they stopped trying. Suppression is a tracking task that keeps the target active. The same mechanism inflates cravings, pre-performance nerves and 02:00 rumination. If you have ever tried to force yourself calm, you have felt it.

**Acceptance is not approval**
In acceptance and commitment therapy, supported by hundreds of trials across anxiety, pain and performance (Hayes et al. 2006), acceptance means making room for the feeling without obeying or fighting it. Pain studies show it plainly: Masedo and Esteve (2007) found acceptance instructions beat suppression on pain tolerance. The feeling is allowed to exist; it just does not get the steering wheel.

**Defusion, ten seconds, any time**
Change "I can't do this" to "I am having the thought that I can't do this." Say it slowly. You have moved from being inside the thought to looking at it. Thoughts are events in the mind, not orders. This one move works on cravings, doubts and catastrophes alike.

**The control sort, two minutes, when gripped**
Two columns. Mine: effort, preparation, response, the next action. Not mine: outcomes, other people's opinions, timing, the past. Everything in the second column gets one deliberate exhale and the sentence: not mine to carry; mine is the next action. Then take it. The Stoics ran their whole system on this sort, and the Wisdom shelf's lesson on Epictetus has the original.

**The evening release, three minutes, after the check-in**
Name the one thing still gripping you. Sort it: what part is yours? Write tomorrow's single action for it. Three physiological sighs, and the line: day closed, handled what was mine. Carrying the day into bed is a sleep tax; the release pays it before the pillow.

**Where this meets manifestation**
Lesson 2's ritual ends with act, then release the outcome. Now you have the release half: full effort on the action, open hands on the result. Maximum agency, minimum grip, is what the traditions were pointing at.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$The grip itself, on thoughts, outcomes and how things should be, is a measurable performance and sleep tax. Acceptance and defusion release it without lowering your standards.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Catch one sticky thought today and run the defusion phrasing on it.$v$, $v$Do the two-column control sort on whatever is gripping you right now.$v$, $v$Tonight after the check-in: the three-minute evening release.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Suppressing a thought rebounds; fighting feeds the fire.$v$, $v$Defusion: I am having the thought that, turns an order into an observable event.$v$, $v$Sort control ruthlessly: full effort on yours, one exhale on the rest.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "What does thought suppression do, in Wegner's research?", "choices": ["Nothing measurable", "Removes the thought permanently", "Increases the thought's frequency, with a rebound afterwards"], "correct": 2, "explain": "Monitoring for the forbidden thought keeps it active. Suppression is the fuel, not the extinguisher."}, {"q": "Defusion changes I can't do this into…", "choices": ["I am having the thought that I can't do this", "I will never think this again", "I definitely can do this"], "correct": 0, "explain": "The rephrasing creates distance: the thought becomes an observable event instead of a command. No forced positivity."}, {"q": "Letting go, done right, means…", "choices": ["Lowering effort and standards", "Full effort on what is yours, released grip on outcomes", "Avoiding hard situations"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Maximum agency on the controllable, open hands on the rest. Surrender as strategy, not resignation."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Hayes, S. C., Luoma, J. B., Bond, F. W., Masuda, A. & Lillis, J.", "title": "Acceptance and commitment therapy: model, processes and outcomes (Behav. Res. Ther.)", "year": 2006}, {"author": "Wegner, D. M.", "title": "Ironic processes of mental control (Psychol. Rev.)", "year": 1994}, {"author": "Masedo, A. I. & Esteve, M. R.", "title": "Effects of suppression, acceptance and spontaneous coping on pain tolerance, pain intensity and distress (Behav. Res. Ther.)", "year": 2007}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$What are you gripping right now that is not yours to carry, and what is the one part of it that is?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$A week of the evening release. What did you stop taking to bed?$v$, practice_minutes = 3, lesson_number = 9, display_order = 9, course_role = $v$protocol$v$, read_time_min = 3, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'letting-go';

UPDATE public.vault_articles SET title = $v$Recap: Your Inner Protocol$v$, subtitle = $v$The nine lessons as one daily and weekly rhythm$v$, summary = $v$The tools only compound on a schedule. This recap folds attention, identity, state and release into a fifteen-minute daily rhythm and a twenty-minute weekly review.$v$, evidence_tier = $v$promising$v$, protocol = $v${"duration": "15 min a day; 20 min a week", "frequency": "Daily and weekly", "intensity": "Morning prime, pre-block state, evening bank and release; weekly review", "prerequisites": "Lessons 1 to 9"}$v$::jsonb, benefits = ARRAY[$v$One page that operationalises the whole shelf$v$, $v$A daily rhythm that costs about fifteen minutes$v$, $v$A weekly review that keeps the votes honest$v$], risks = ARRAY[$v$Install it in pieces; the whole rhythm on day one is the kind of programme people abandon by Thursday$v$, $v$The rhythm is a container for the practices; the evidence sits in the lessons$v$], body_md = $v$**Four themes**
Nine lessons, four themes: prime attention, build identity, manage state, release the rest. Here they become a schedule.

**Morning, five minutes: prime**
Read the I am statement from lesson 8; the day's first identity vote is remembering what you are voting for. Two minutes of vision on your primary desire, lesson 2's ritual, and name today's same-day action. If the day holds a known battle, a sixty-second WOOP if-then from lesson 3.

**Before the hard block, five minutes: state and rehearsal**
The peak-state ritual from lesson 5: breath, posture, one track, one line of meaning. Three minutes of process imagery including the hardest moment, lesson 4. One named coach instruction from lesson 7: your name, first set, nothing else.

**During the day, no extra minutes**
Savour one good moment for thirty seconds, lesson 6. Defuse one sticky thought, lesson 9: I am having the thought that.

**Evening, five minutes: bank and release**
Check in and lock the day, the identity vote made visible. Three good things with the why, lesson 6. The evening release from lesson 9: sort what is yours, one action for tomorrow, three sighs, day closed.

**The weekly review, twenty minutes, same day each week**
Votes: what did this week's actions vote for, and does the tally match the I am? Values: hours and energy against claimed values, lesson 8, one gap to close. Energy: chargers up, drains down, one drain deleted, lesson 5. WOOP the coming week's biggest wish, obstacle included, lesson 3. Express one gratitude to a real person, lesson 6.

**Install it in pieces**
Week one, the evening five minutes. Week two, add the morning. Week three, the pre-block ritual. Week four, the review. A rhythm added all at once is abandoned by Thursday.

**The honest core, once more**
Nothing on this shelf asked you to believe anything unprovable. Primed attention finds openings. Identity votes compound. State is physiology, and physiology is trainable. Effort belongs to you; outcomes only partly. Run the rhythm for four weeks, and the person running it becomes the system.$v$, why_it_matters = $v$Tools that are not scheduled do not compound. Fifteen deliberate minutes a day and one weekly review turn nine lessons of theory into a running inner operating system.$v$, try_today = ARRAY[$v$Put the evening five minutes in the calendar for the next seven days; add the morning in week two.$v$, $v$Pick the weekly review slot, twenty minutes, same day, and set it recurring.$v$, $v$Run tonight's evening block and rate the day from one to ten against a normal day.$v$], key_takeaways = ARRAY[$v$Four themes: prime attention, build identity, manage state, release the rest.$v$, $v$About fifteen minutes a day: morning prime, pre-block state, evening bank and release.$v$, $v$The weekly review keeps the votes, the values and the energy honestly audited.$v$], quiz = $v$[{"q": "The four themes of the inner protocol are…", "choices": ["Wish, wait, receive, repeat", "Diet, cardio, lifting, sleep", "Prime attention, build identity, manage state, release the rest"], "correct": 2, "explain": "Every lesson maps to one of the four; together they make the daily and weekly rhythm."}, {"q": "Why does the evening end with both gratitude and release?", "choices": ["Gratitude retrains the filter on wins; release drops the residue so it does not tax sleep", "To make journaling longer", "Tradition"], "correct": 0, "explain": "Bank what worked, put down what grips, then sleep clean. They are complementary moves."}, {"q": "What makes the tools on this shelf compound?", "choices": ["Believing in them strongly", "Running them on a schedule until the rhythm is automatic", "Re-reading the lessons"], "correct": 1, "explain": "Fifteen minutes daily and twenty weekly, installed in pieces, is what turns techniques into a system."}]$v$::jsonb, references_json = $v$[{"author": "Wood, W. & Neal, D. T.", "title": "A new look at habits and the habit-goal interface (Psychol. Rev.)", "year": 2007}, {"author": "Oettingen, G.", "title": "Rethinking Positive Thinking: Inside the New Science of Motivation", "year": 2014}, {"author": "Kross, E.", "title": "Chatter: The Voice in Our Head, Why It Matters, and How to Harness It", "year": 2021}]$v$::jsonb, reflect_prompt = $v$Which of the four themes is the one you would drop first on a bad week, and what does that tell you?$v$, integrate_prompt = $v$Four weeks of the rhythm, installed in pieces. Which five minutes earned their place, and which did you quietly stop?$v$, practice_minutes = 15, lesson_number = 10, display_order = 10, course_role = $v$recap$v$, read_time_min = 3, updated_at = now() WHERE slug = 'inner-work-recap';
